# C#

## Style

- Clear names; prefer explicit over clever.
- New code: readable plain C#; match existing file style when editing.
- Avoid mega-classes; split by responsibility (data / sim / view / net).

## Unity-specific C#

- Prefer composing small components/services over inheritance trees.
- Cache references thoughtfully; no per-frame `GetComponent` in hot paths without reason.
- Gameplay-critical motion: drive through physics APIs (`AddForce`, torques, motor joints, velocities) — not `transform` writes on dynamic bodies.
- Keep serializable DTOs/`ScriptableObject` data free of net/session side effects.

## Testability

- Pure functions for validation, binding resolve, damage rules where possible.
- Avoid `FindObjectOfType` as architecture.
- No hidden singletons that own half the game; if a service locator exists, keep it thin and explicit.

## Safety

- Clamp/validate external and networked values at trust boundaries.
- Do not swallow exceptions silently in sim/net paths; fail loud in development.

## Dependencies

- No new NuGet/UPM dependency without a one-line justification in the change notes.
